android: A32 — pace the fps cap by a lower-bound delay, not a vblank grid

`steamcompmgr_should_vblank_window` drops callbacks on a
`vblank_idx % (refresh / target)` grid whenever a fixed-rate panel is in use.
That grid is unrelated to when the guest actually committed, so a title already
at its own ceiling loses a whole cycle whenever its commit lands late or off the
grid: Dishonored reads ~57/s uncapped but ~45-51/s with a 60 target armed. The
VRR branch instead defers on `w->last_commit_first_latch_time + cycle`, a lower
bound anchored to the frame's own latch, so it can never push the rate below
what the guest produced.

Give the static path the same lower-bound delay, and make the limiter's cycle
the exact target frame time (`1/target`) instead of
`AppRefreshCycle * floor(refresh / target)`. The integer divisor both quantised
the cap and mis-capped targets that do not divide the refresh (60 on 90Hz gave
floor(90/60) = 1, i.e. no cap). The VRR path already used `1/target`; both
paths now share it.

No VRR property and no fabricated panel mode: this changes only the pacing
arithmetic of gamescope's own backend, and is a no-op for a real VRR panel.

